  "worked_solution": "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x+1)+1}{x+1})}^{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x+1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
